<v Alex>So Musk thinks no one can replace him.

30
00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:42.660
<v Maya>He talks about loyalty and tech knowledge,

31
00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.560
<v Maya>but Nvidia’s interim CEO is already in place.

32
00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:50.160
<v Alex>And the board’s focus is on the company’s continuity, not the hype.

33
00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.900
<v Maya>That’s the headline, now let’s dig into the market angle.

34
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.580
<v Maya>What’s the real depth behind Musk’s “Michael Jordan” line?

35
00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:03.050
<v Alex>He’s basically saying Jensen’s tech know‑how and team loyalty are irreplaceable.

36
00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:05.240
<v Maya>So he’s framing it like a sports team?

37
00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:10.780
<v Alex>Yeah, and he pointed out a lot of Nvidia staff have higher offers but stay because

38
00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:11.320
<v Alex>of Jensen.

39
00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:12.520
<v Maya>Did anyone else weigh in?

40
00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:18.080
<v Alex>Palmer Luckey jumped in, saying China wants Jensen out and that hurts Nvidia’s

41
00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:18.560
<v Alex>valuation.

42
00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:19.700
<v Maya>Interesting angle.

43
00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:23.500
<v Alex>And the board’s stance is clear—no comeback plan,

44
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.780
<v Alex>just moving forward with the interim CEO.

45
00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:32.280
<v Alex>I think Musk is just throwing hype, calling a CEO a Michael Jordan to stir drama.

46
00:02:32.825 --> 00:02:33.210
<v Alex>Seriously?

47
00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.880
<v Maya>It shows a chunk of the community feels the board's move is risky.

48
00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:41.320
<v Alex>But the board says no comeback plan, so the risk is only in perception.

49
00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.270
<v Maya>True, yet the interim CEO has a solid Microsoft background,

50
00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:47.640
<v Maya>which could stabilize the market.

51
00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:53.120
<v Alex>Still, the loyalty angle—staff staying against higher offers—could hint at deeper

52
00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:53.600
<v Alex>unrest.

53
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:58.820
<v Maya>It sounds like Nvidia is balancing internal morale with external investor

54
00:02:58.820 --> 00:02:59.460
<v Maya>confidence.

55
00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:07.070
<v Maya>15-0 Republican vote, Democrats absent, that’s the kicker, Ars Technica reports.

56
00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.150
<v Alex>So Cruz just ran the show without a quorum.

57
00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:13.320
<v Maya>He said they’d do their job even if the others refuse.

58
00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:14.140
<v Alex>That’s wild.

59
00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:20.260
<v Maya>The nominee is Danielle Thumann, a former legal advisor to FCC chair Brendan Carr.

60
00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.080
<v Alex>She supposedly gave false testimony about that role.

61
00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:28.300
<v Maya>Republicans hope to confirm her before Senate flips in January.

62
00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:29.020
<v Alex>Makes sense.

63
00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.060
<v Maya>And that’s all we know from the article.

64
00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.040
<v Alex>What about the actual vetting process that went on before this vote?

65
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:39.920
<v Maya>The committee reviews the nominee’s background, then votes; if they pass,

66
00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:41.520
<v Maya>the full Senate needs a majority.

67
00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.860
<v Alex>So they cut straight to the final step?

68
00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.630
<v Maya>Yeah, they skipped the usual committee debate because the Democrats didn't show up.

69
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.680
<v Alex>Did anyone try to stop the quorum?

70
00:03:51.115 --> 00:03:54.420
<v Maya>The Democrats said they’d prevent a quorum, but they stayed away.

71
00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.780
<v Alex>How does that affect the overall confirmation?

72
00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:00.580
<v Maya>Without a quorum, the committee can still move forward,

73
00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:02.820
<v Maya>but the full Senate still has to approve later.

74
00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:06.460
<v Maya>This feels like a political stunt, not a real vetting.

75
00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:08.580
<v Alex>But the committee still did its job.

76
00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:12.080
<v Maya>So the real question is, what happens in the full Senate?

77
00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:15.500
<v Alex>Honestly, it's just a formality if they get a quorum.

78
00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:19.620
<v Maya>I think it signals that the commission can ignore dissenting voices.

79
00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:23.410
<v Alex>And that the confirmation might slip through before the power shift.

80
00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:25.470
<v Maya>That’s the point, right?

81
00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:26.220
<v Maya>They’re moving fast.

82
00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:28.900
<v Alex>I guess that’s the reality of the process.

83
00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.540
<v Alex>Maya, what did Ukraine do to that bridge?

84
00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:36.320
<v Maya>They hit a steel arch with missiles and drones, no Starlink.

85
00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:37.820
<v Alex>No Starlink?

86
00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:38.960
<v Alex>How did they guide the shots?

87
00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:42.420
<v Maya>They used mesh‑radio drones as a relay network.

88
00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:43.200
<v Alex>Huh, that's wild.

89
00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:46.940
<v Maya>Russian air defenses were reportedly overwhelmed first.

90
00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:48.380
<v Alex>So the drones set the stage?

91
00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:51.710
<v Maya>Yes, then the Flamingo cruise missile finished the job.

92
00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:57.750
<v Alex>It was a coordinated night attack, November 15‑16, Ars Technica reports.

93
00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:00.640
<v Maya>What exactly does that mesh‑radio tech do?

94
00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:03.860
<v Alex>Think of each drone as a hop‑scotch board,

95
00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:07.200
<v Alex>passing signals to the missile until it finally reaches the target.

96
00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.360
<v Maya>So the drones themselves are the link, not the operator?

97
00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:15.980
<v Alex>Right, the human stays off the line; the relay chain keeps the missile fed until it

98
00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:16.670
<v Alex>hits the arch.

99
00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.420
<v Maya>And Russia started using the same trick on Ukrainian cities?

100
00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:24.260
<v Alex>Yeah, they reversed the play, turning the technology against them.

101
00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:25.420
<v Maya>Who’s saying this, exactly?

102
00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.510
<v Alex>The Financial Times broke it down,

103
00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:32.880
<v Alex>citing how Ukrainian forces adapted the Russian battlefield innovation.

104
00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:35.500
<v Alex>That relay trick flips the battlefield, huh.

105
00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:38.740
<v Maya>But it also shows how tech can be turned on its own side.

106
00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.200
<v Alex>The drones are like a chain—no human in the middle, just packets hopping.

107
00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:46.520
<v Maya>So the same method Russia later used on cities?

108
00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:51.720
<v Alex>It suggests a new layer of vulnerability, but no one says what else could break.

109
00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.960
<v Maya>In any case, the cost of buying that tech or defending against it is up in the air.

110
00:05:57.450 --> 00:05:59.860
<v Alex>And the question is, who pays for the next wave?

111
00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:03.000
<v Maya>The market will decide, and the price tag will follow.

112
00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:09.520
<v Maya>Microsoft’s Nadella says AI agents will rework how we pay for web traffic and

113
00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:11.840
<v Maya>transactions, according to TechCrunch.

114
00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:13.770
<v Alex>That sounds like a big shift.

115
00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:16.540
<v Maya>He compared it to when Google overtook Yahoo,

116
00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:19.780
<v Maya>and said the current ad‑based models will get reshuffled.

117
00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:21.580
<v Alex>So it’s a cost‑per‑click push?

118
00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:26.160
<v Maya>He added that if you don’t embrace agents, someone else will.

119
00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.440
<v Maya>Amazon’s block on agents might open the door for competitors.

120
00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.840
<v Alex>Shopify could leap ahead if it lets agents in.

121
00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:37.380
<v Maya>That’s the crux—companies that lock out agents risk losing market share.

122
00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:42.720
<v Alex>It’s a gamble, but the tech‑side view is that agents could change the economics of

123
00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:43.120
<v Alex>the web.

124
00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:45.420
<v Alex>What exactly did he mean by “agent”?

125
00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:50.610
<v Maya>He was talking about software agents that can browse the web on behalf of users,

126
00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:53.500
<v Maya>pulling data and making purchases automatically.

127
00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:55.020
<v Alex>So like a virtual shopper?

128
00:06:55.540 --> 00:07:00.200
<v Maya>Yeah, but the point is they change who pays for traffic—shifting from eyeball ads

129
00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:02.540
<v Maya>to cost‑per‑click or transaction fees.

130
00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:04.220
<v Alex>He said Amazon blocks them?

131
00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:08.800
<v Maya>Yes, Amazon blocks agents from its site, so if they stay shut,

132
00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:12.910
<v Maya>a platform like Shopify could open its doors and attract those agents,

133
00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:13.760
<v Maya>gaining a foothold.

134
00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:14.480
<v Alex>Is that all?

135
00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:19.540
<v Maya>He added that if a company resists agents, someone else will win the race,

136
00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:23.710
<v Maya>just like Google overtook Yahoo when the web moved from portals to search.

137
00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:27.080
<v Maya>If Amazon keeps locking out agents,

138
00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:30.340
<v Maya>it’s a risk that Shopify could leap ahead by opening the door.

139
00:07:30.813 --> 00:07:35.900
<v Alex>But the tech side worries: agents could break the current ad models before anyone

140
00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:36.480
<v Alex>profits.

141
00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:40.780
<v Maya>Do you think the shift will really favor transaction fees over eyeballs?

142
00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:45.900
<v Alex>Maybe, but the first wave of agents might still be messy and buggy—hard to trust

143
00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:46.540
<v Alex>them to pay.

144
00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:50.720
<v Maya>Hold on, didn’t Nadella say the agents would change who pays for traffic?

145
00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:56.120
<v Alex>It sounds like a new game, but only if the agents actually work reliably; otherwise

146
00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:57.290
<v Alex>the old model sticks.

147
00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:02.800
<v Maya>So the real gamble is whether platforms will let the agents in or block them and

148
00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:03.420
<v Maya>lose ground.

149
00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:08.120
<v Alex>In the end, it’s a test of whether the web’s economics can adapt to automated

150
00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:08.660
<v Alex>shoppers.

151
00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:12.200
<v Alex>That’s a big pivot, huh?

152
00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:17.140
<v Alex>Nvidia just cut off H200 shipments after getting clearance.

153
00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:22.080
<v Alex>The interim CEO said they’re not shipping licensed Hopper data center products to

154
00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:22.500
<v Alex>China.

155
00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:23.690
<v Maya>No way.

156
00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:25.000
<v Maya>They had approval last December.

157
00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:29.300
<v Alex>The Verge says Colette Kress made the call on earnings.

158
00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:32.320
<v Alex>She didn’t explain why or if they’ll touch China later.

159
00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:34.510
<v Maya>I think it’s a political move.

160
00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:37.120
<v Maya>The article doesn’t say, but the timing matters.

161
00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:40.020
<v Alex>Funny, they fought for it, then backpedal.

162
00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:40.840
<v Alex>What’s the next step?

163
00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:44.880
<v Maya>Might mean no more sales, but could keep other business open.

164
00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:46.160
<v Maya>The article stops short.

165
00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:50.360
<v Alex>So they’re pulling back on the big chips, but nothing on other products.

166
00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:51.440
<v Maya>Right, that’s the take.

167
00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:52.700
<v Maya>What’s the funding angle?

168
00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:56.740
<v Alex>They’re not shipping any licensed Hopper data‑center chips,

169
00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:59.180
<v Alex>so the revenue stream from China is gone.

170
00:08:59.380 --> 00:08:59.780
<v Maya>No way.

171
00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:04.640
<v Alex>The article says Colette Kress made that call on the earnings call,

172
00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:07.040
<v Alex>but didn’t explain why she stopped the shipments.

173
00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:07.940
<v Maya>So it’s a mystery?

174
00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:11.410
<v Alex>The white house had given clearance last December,

175
00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:13.770
<v Alex>so the shutdown is a deliberate pullback.

176
00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:15.200
<v Maya>Any hint on future deals?

177
00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:19.920
<v Alex>No mention of other business, just that the H200 shipments are halted.

178
00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:21.400
<v Alex>That’s a big pivot, huh?

179
00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:22.000
<v Maya>No way.

180
00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:24.690
<v Maya>Hold on, you’re reading politics into it.

181
00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:25.460
<v Maya>That’s the take.

182
00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:28.400
<v Alex>I’m curious if they’ll keep other business open.

183
00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:29.680
<v Maya>They might.

184
00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:30.640
<v Maya>That’s the bottom line.

185
00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:35.160
<v Maya>Nvidia’s H200 chips out of China, all done.

186
00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:37.440
<v Alex>Wait, how did they even get a loophole?

187
00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:39.560
<v Maya>Huang brokered a U.S.

188
00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:42.640
<v Maya>route so China could pay a 25% tariff.

189
00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:43.960
<v Alex>That’s a weird compromise.

190
00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:47.700
<v Maya>The board’s new committee just formed to watch export rules.

191
00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:51.800
<v Alex>So no more H200s, but top‑line stays the same?

192
00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:57.480
<v Maya>Jefferies says revenue could drop $44‑$62 billion next year.

193
00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:01.040
<v Alex>But they claim backlog of demand keeps revenue flat.

194
00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:06.580
<v Maya>Chinese labs still training on Grace Blackwell chips, but sales to them now barred.

195
00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:10.700
<v Alex>What’s the deal with that export‑control guy Dan Fisher‑Owens?

196
00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:15.960
<v Maya>He told us that any data center wanting Nvidia chips must prove it isn’t serving

197
00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:18.980
<v Maya>Chinese customers; otherwise they’ll be blocked.

198
00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:21.760
<v Alex>So they’re actually going to audit every buyer?

199
00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:25.870
<v Maya>That’s the idea—certification of non‑China service,

200
00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:30.420
<v Maya>or they lose access to the H200 and even the Grace Blackwell line.

201
00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:31.000
<v Alex>“Says who?”

202
00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:35.860
<v Maya>The attorney’s comment came from a TechCrunch interview; it’s a standard compliance

203
00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:37.180
<v Maya>check under the new rules.

204
00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:39.360
<v Alex>Sounds like a bureaucratic maze.

205
00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:44.800
<v Maya>Plus, the board’s new committee is overseeing national‑security, export‑control,

206
00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:47.800
<v Maya>and global‑trade risks, so they’re tightening the screws.

207
00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:53.480
<v Alex>Meaning Nvidia’s still keeping the top‑line, but it’s a different customer base now.

208
00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:57.260
<v Maya>I think they’re just trimming a risky line but keeping the cash flow.

209
00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:00.720
<v Alex>That sounds like a band‑aid, not a real solution.

210
00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:04.900
<v Maya>The board’s committee shows they’re tightening controls, not cutting corners.

211
00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:05.900
<v Alex>Tightening?

212
00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:08.240
<v Alex>Sounds more like a bureaucratic nightmare.

213
00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:12.300
<v Maya>The backlog of demand could keep revenue flat, even if China’s out.

214
00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:14.680
<v Alex>But who’s buying those chips now?

215
00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:15.800
<v Alex>The market is shrinking.

216
00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:20.280
<v Maya>Maybe it’s a pivot to other regions—Singapore, Malaysia, Thailand.

217
00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:25.400
<v Alex>If they can’t certify non‑China use, they’ll lose those customers,

218
00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:26.520
<v Alex>so it’s a real hit.

219
00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:35.860
<v Alex>Nvidia’s private stake is now $65.5 billion, up from $22 billion last January.

220
00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:38.060
<v Maya>That’s a jump of almost three times.

221
00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:38.504
<v Alex>Huh.

222
00:11:38.504 --> 00:11:39.400
<v Maya>Who’s driving that?

223
00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:43.980
<v Alex>Jensen Huang pushed a $16 billion equity spree last quarter.

224
00:11:44.054 --> 00:11:45.070
<v Maya>And she’s stepping out?

225
00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:49.440
<v Alex>Interim CEO plus a new permanent leader could cool the fire.

226
00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:51.540
<v Maya>So the VC pipeline might shrink.

227
00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:52.760
<v Maya>What about the guarantees?

228
00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:57.800
<v Alex>They poured $118 billion into guarantees,

229
00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:02.380
<v Alex>$105 billion of that for OpenAI’s Ohio center.

230
00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:03.440
<v Maya>That’s a lot of cash.

231
00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:05.680
<v Alex>Yeah, it feels like a safety net.

232
00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:07.180
<v Maya>Who’s saying this might slow?

233
00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:12.310
<v Alex>Kress warned Huang’s exit could make investors go more cautious, smaller checks.

234
00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:14.520
<v Maya>So the pipeline might shrink.

235
00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:17.790
<v Alex>And the 350 startup count stays the same?

236
00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:23.010
<v Maya>They’ve backed almost 350, but no new round numbers after the spike.

237
00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:25.630
<v Alex>Sounds like a firework, but only a flicker.

238
00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:27.580
<v Maya>Feels like a sprint that might stall.

239
00:12:27.595 --> 00:12:27.900
<v Alex>Huh.

240
00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:30.720
<v Maya>If the checks shrink, the pipeline will thin.

241
00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:33.960
<v Alex>Right, but they still have 350 names.

242
00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:37.420
<v Maya>But without the big pushes, those names might not grow.

243
00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:40.490
<v Alex>Exactly, and the guarantees keep the heat alive.

244
00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:44.060
<v Maya>It seems the next CEO will have to balance fire and safety.

245
00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:49.320
<v Maya>Hey Alex, any idea why Anthropic shot its price to $83?

246
00:12:50.140 --> 00:12:54.800
<v Alex>They pushed the offer up, selling 980 million shares,

247
00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:57.640
<v Alex>not the 825 million they’d planned.

248
00:12:58.130 --> 00:12:59.820
<v Maya>That’s more than the range they said, right?

249
00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:03.120
<v Alex>Yeah, over the $70‑$78 band.

250
00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:08.880
<v Maya>And they’re pulling in about $81.3 billion, beating SpaceX’s record.

251
00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:11.530
<v Alex>81.3 billion, huh?

252
00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:13.140
<v Alex>That’s a lot of cash for a startup.

253
00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:19.440
<v Maya>Plus, Amazon, Google, Microsoft, Nvidia, and AMD are all in the mix,

254
00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:21.140
<v Maya>each investing $26 billion.

255
00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:24.380
<v Alex>So the big players back them—makes sense.

256
00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:29.240
<v Alex>What about the tech side—how does Claude actually pile up that revenue bump?

257
00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:34.800
<v Maya>Claude Code drove 1,200% jump in the last nine months,

258
00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:37.120
<v Maya>and that’s the tool customers love for coding.

259
00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:41.900
<v Maya>Right, the run‑rate revenue is about $81 billion, not a trillion.

260
00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:42.280
<v Alex>That’s huge.

261
00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:46.220
<v Maya>Each co‑founder sold 3.38 million shares,

262
00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:51.160
<v Maya>netting $1.96 billion—shows insiders backing the price.

263
00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:54.980
<v Alex>Funny how the founders cash out while the company goes public.

264
00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:59.440
<v Maya>And the big techs—Amazon, Google, Microsoft, Nvidia,

265
00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:02.940
<v Maya>AMD—each poured $26 billion into the IPO.

266
00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:03.840
<v Maya>That's wild.

267
00:14:04.731 --> 00:14:08.060
<v Alex>But the price hike just means they over‑estimated demand.

268
00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:13.300
<v Maya>The $26 billion from each big tech feels like a vote of confidence, though.

269
00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:17.920
<v Alex>Might just be a hedge; they’re buying into a future that might not deliver.

270
00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:24.090
<v Maya>The 1,200% revenue jump on Claude Code shows customers love it,

271
00:14:24.090 --> 00:14:25.380
<v Maya>so demand could be real.

272
00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:31.900
<v Alex>Still, the $104 billion loss in nine months is a red flag about sustainability.

273
00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:37.200
<v Maya>If the founders are cashing out, maybe they see a quick exit, not a long‑term play.

274
00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:41.730
<v Alex>I think the real test is whether the tech holds up when the market tightens.

275
00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:46.710
<v Alex>Anthropic is about to go public, and people are lining up.

276
00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:49.760
<v Maya>According to CNBC, that’s the headline.

277
00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:53.000
<v Alex>The hype is real, but the mechanics are still murky.

278
00:14:53.510 --> 00:14:58.240
<v Maya>Retail could grab up to 20% of the 825 million shares.

279
00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:03.760
<v Alex>Dan Shetty drained his Roth IRA for $12,000 in hopes of a stake.

280
00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:09.780
<v Maya>Vanda Research projects $250 million of retail net buying—double SpaceX.

281
00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:16.920
<v Alex>The IPO price range is $70–$78, but sources say it might go higher.

282
00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:21.470
<v Maya>Joe Pak warns allocations are tight and macro conditions are tough.

283
00:15:21.935 --> 00:15:24.900
<v Maya>What’s still nagging people after the basics hit the airwaves?

284
00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:26.660
<v Alex>No way, the numbers are wild.

285
00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:29.260
<v Maya>How big is the company actually?

286
00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:36.320
<v Alex>It’s less than six years old, but it’s already pitching a $1.405 trillion valuation

287
00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:39.780
<v Alex>and wants to lift $64.4 billion.

288
00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:41.180
<v Maya>That’s a monster.

289
00:15:41.800 --> 00:15:49.320
<v Alex>If the demand hits, it could pull in more than SpaceX’s $85.7 billion IPO; that’s

290
00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:51.020
<v Alex>the only other comparable beast.

291
00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:52.280
<v Maya>So the hype could be real.

292
00:15:52.790 --> 00:15:55.900
<v Alex>I think the hype is mostly about the numbers, not the tech.

293
00:15:56.450 --> 00:15:59.280
<v Maya>It’s the retail pull, that’s the story for the market.

294
00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:01.000
<v Alex>No way, 20% allocation?

295
00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:04.080
<v Maya>That’s a lot of demand, but allocations stay tight.

296
00:16:04.700 --> 00:16:07.690
<v Alex>If it eclipses SpaceX, the valuation could jump.

297
00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:10.360
<v Maya>But the price might still be above the range.

298
00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:12.980
<v Alex>So it’s a gamble for the average investor.

299
00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:16.340
<v Maya>Precisely, the upside is huge, the risk is real.

300
00:16:17.960 --> 00:16:20.860
<v Alex>You're listening to First Tick, from Bits and Tickers.

301
00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:22.520
<v Alex>That's about the halfway mark.

302
00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:23.800
<v Alex>Still to come.

303
00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:29.090
<v Alex>Palo Alto inks $4.5 billion deal for AI startup Applied Compute,

304
00:16:29.090 --> 00:16:31.630
<v Alex>shares pop 7% on earnings.

305
00:16:31.630 --> 00:16:34.620
<v Alex>Microsoft backs Nvidia’s interim boss.

306
00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:41.040
<v Alex>And India’s Humyn Labs raises $47M to feed US robotics companies the data they need.

307
00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:44.910
<v Maya>And if you just joined us, I'm Maya, that's Alex,

308
00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:47.040
<v Maya>and every story is linked in the show notes.

309
00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:53.340
<v Maya>Hot deal, Palo Alto just snapped up Applied Compute for $4.5 billion.

310
00:16:54.270 --> 00:16:58.440
<v Alex>The startup helps run custom AI models, not just security.

311
00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:02.120
<v Maya>They say it could dwarf their own cybersecurity business.

312
00:17:02.120 --> 00:17:02.800
<v Alex>Makes sense.

313
00:17:03.470 --> 00:17:06.920
<v Maya>Shares jumped 7% after the earnings beat and the deal.

314
00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:14.690
<v Alex>Quarterly ARR for next‑gen security hit $9.73 billion, up 66%.

315
00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:23.000
<v Maya>CEO Nikesh Arora pushed revenue guidance up to $14.52‑$14.57 billion.

316
00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:27.880
<v Alex>He also warned about tens of thousands of virtual patches needed.

317
00:17:28.270 --> 00:17:32.060
<v Maya>Looks like AI is turning into a new revenue stream for cyber firms.

318
00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:35.380
<v Alex>What exactly does Applied Compute do for the models?

319
00:17:35.790 --> 00:17:39.310
<v Maya>They help companies run and tweak their own AI models,

320
00:17:39.310 --> 00:17:41.980
<v Maya>keeping data on‑prem or in private clouds.

321
00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:44.160
<v Alex>Why would a cyber firm buy that?

322
00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:48.780
<v Maya>The CEO said it could be “bigger than what we do” in security—like a new revenue

323
00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:49.260
<v Maya>engine.

324
00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:49.840
<v Alex>Makes sense.

325
00:17:50.510 --> 00:17:53.800
<v Maya>And it’s all‑stock for $4.5 billion,

326
00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:57.700
<v Maya>so Palo Alto keeps cash flow intact while adding AI chops.

327
00:17:58.300 --> 00:17:59.880
<v Alex>How much patching are we looking at?

328
00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:03.560
<v Maya>Tens of thousands of virtual patches by year‑end,

329
00:18:03.560 --> 00:18:05.640
<v Maya>each taking three to nine months to implement.

330
00:18:06.190 --> 00:18:07.780
<v Maya>This could be a game‑changer for them.

331
00:18:08.180 --> 00:18:11.320
<v Alex>Maybe, but only if the AI side actually delivers.

332
00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:16.780
<v Maya>They’re betting that custom AI models will outpace their current security revenue.

333
00:18:17.280 --> 00:18:21.520
<v Alex>And it’s all‑stock, so they’re not burning cash—just swapping equity for tech.

334
00:18:22.120 --> 00:18:26.860
<v Maya>The patch backlog they mentioned—tens of thousands—could be a real operational

335
00:18:26.860 --> 00:18:27.300
<v Maya>headache.

336
00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:32.680
<v Alex>I think the real test will be how quickly they can integrate those models into

337
00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:33.320
<v Alex>their platform.

338
00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:37.120
<v Maya>If they nail it, the next‑gen security numbers will keep climbing.

339
00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:40.930
<v Alex>Then it’s just a matter of scaling the new engine fast enough.

340
00:18:42.610 --> 00:18:45.100
<v Alex>Colette Kress just stepped in after Jensen Huang.

341
00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:49.020
<v Maya>Nadella said he trusts her, citing their long Microsoft ties.

342
00:18:49.590 --> 00:18:51.380
<v Alex>“All tragic, but I’ll give him space.”

343
00:18:51.950 --> 00:18:54.960
<v Maya>He also compared founders to gods, then said they die.

344
00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:56.040
<v Alex>No way, that’s a twist.

345
00:18:56.530 --> 00:18:57.980
<v Maya>He joked about Bill Gates too.

346
00:18:58.420 --> 00:19:00.840
<v Alex>So the tech big‑shot keeps the partnership steady.

347
00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:03.280
<v Maya>And that’s what matters to market watchers.

348
00:19:03.940 --> 00:19:06.280
<v Maya>What’s the deal with Colette’s background at Microsoft?

349
00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:10.260
<v Alex>She’s been on the cloud team for years, leads AI infra.

350
00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:11.620
<v Maya>So she knows Nvidia’s stack?

351
00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:13.830
<v Alex>Not the tech, but the people.

352
00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:15.820
<v Alex>She worked with the same execs he did.

353
00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:16.980
<v Maya>That explains the trust.

354
00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:20.470
<v Alex>And Nadella said he’ll stay in touch with the same contacts.

355
00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:22.680
<v Maya>So it’s a people‑centric hand‑off.

356
00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:25.980
<v Alex>Yeah, it feels like a familiar hand pulling the wheel.

357
00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:29.480
<v Alex>Colette’s tech know‑how is shallow, but people are solid.

358
00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:32.560
<v Maya>The trust is purely people‑centric, not tech.

359
00:19:32.680 --> 00:19:33.760
<v Alex>No way, it’s a hand‑shake.

360
00:19:33.970 --> 00:19:35.000
<v Maya>So the partnership stays?

361
00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:37.220
<v Alex>It’s the same execs, same vibe.

362
00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:38.380
<v Maya>That’s the market’s comfort.

363
00:19:38.910 --> 00:19:40.680
<v Alex>Still, the disruption risk lingers.

364
00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:43.720
<v Maya>It’s a relief for investors, not a revelation.

365
00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:51.480
<v Maya>Humyn Labs just raised $47 million, and 90% of that cash is meant to feed U.S.

366
00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:52.780
<v Maya>robotics firms.

367
00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:58.180
<v Alex>So they’re buying data from people in their homes and turning it into AI training

368
00:19:58.180 --> 00:19:58.700
<v Alex>sets.

369
00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:05.170
<v Maya>Exactly, and the $26 million equity comes from Accel and a bunch of other names,

370
00:20:05.170 --> 00:20:08.440
<v Maya>plus $21 million in debt from Lighthouse Canton.

371
00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:09.440
<v Alex>That’s a lot of money.

372
00:20:09.930 --> 00:20:12.680
<v Maya>They’ve only got 12 paying customers,

373
00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:16.180
<v Maya>but already pulled in $10 million in revenue in half a year.

374
00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:21.120
<v Alex>And they’re paying workers daily while waiting 30–60 days for clients.

375
00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:23.940
<v Maya>That explains why the venture debt is a thing.

376
00:20:24.060 --> 00:20:24.260
<v Alex>Right.

377
00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:29.420
<v Maya>So the big surprise is the scale of the debt‑equity mix for a startup still

378
00:20:29.420 --> 00:20:30.900
<v Maya>hustling to get cash flow.

379
00:20:31.450 --> 00:20:33.500
<v Alex>What’s the trick behind their two‑camera setup?

380
00:20:33.860 --> 00:20:38.900
<v Maya>They’ve built a dual‑camera rig that captures people doing normal chores; then

381
00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:42.160
<v Maya>their software tags hand moves, object interactions,

382
00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:45.120
<v Maya>and turns the raw clips into labeled data.

383
00:20:45.127 --> 00:20:46.320
<v Alex>That’s a lot of detail.

384
00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:50.580
<v Maya>Yeah, and they run this across more than 20 countries, with Brazil,

385
00:20:50.580 --> 00:20:53.560
<v Maya>India and South Korea as the main hubs,

386
00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:56.820
<v Maya>pulling from a network that grew out of their earlier studio.

387
00:20:57.340 --> 00:20:59.400
<v Alex>So the founders came from a game studio?

388
00:20:59.740 --> 00:21:05.000
<v Maya>Right, Manish Agarwal was the former CEO of a gaming firm and Ishank Gupta had a

389
00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:10.590
<v Maya>stint at AB InBev; they spun off Kratos Studios, now KGeN,

390
00:21:10.590 --> 00:21:13.500
<v Maya>which already had a contributor network that feeds Humyn Labs.

391
00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:15.660
<v Alex>That explains the data pipeline.

392
00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:20.730
<v Maya>And on the money side, they’ve hit $10 million in revenue in six months,

393
00:21:20.730 --> 00:21:26.170
<v Maya>with another $20 million in signed deals and another $20 million still pending,

394
00:21:26.170 --> 00:21:28.360
<v Maya>which is why the debt component is so prominent.

395
00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:29.520
<v Maya>That’s a heavy debt.

396
00:21:29.520 --> 00:21:30.640
<v Alex>Interesting debt mix.

397
00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:35.680
<v Maya>I think the debt shows they’re cash‑tight, but it also signals investor confidence.

398
00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:39.180
<v Alex>But the equity was huge, so maybe they’re just hedging.

399
00:21:39.620 --> 00:21:43.160
<v Maya>They only have 12 customers, so the revenue stream feels shaky.

400
00:21:43.280 --> 00:21:44.020
<v Alex>Huh, that’s a risk.

401
00:21:44.470 --> 00:21:46.480
<v Maya>My read is they’re betting on a data boom,

402
00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:51.600
<v Maya>but their model relies on daily payments to workers and long customer terms.

403
00:21:52.130 --> 00:21:55.620
<v Alex>It sounds like they’re building a bridge between labor and AI,

404
00:21:55.620 --> 00:21:59.140
<v Alex>but whether that bridge holds up remains to be seen.

405
00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:02.840
<v Alex>Hey Maya, did you see the IPO buzz?

406
00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:05.840
<v Maya>Yeah, Jaan Tallinn's stake could hit tens of billions.

407
00:22:06.620 --> 00:22:09.450
<v Alex>He’s the same guy who warned AI might end us.

408
00:22:10.200 --> 00:22:12.810
<v Maya>Right, he sold Skype, now betting on Anthropic.

409
00:22:13.350 --> 00:22:17.360
<v Alex>He invested 124 million before Claude even launched.

410
00:22:17.900 --> 00:22:21.200
<v Maya>Anthropic’s valuation could hit about 1.4 trillion.

411
00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:22.060
<v Alex>That’s insane.

412
00:22:22.870 --> 00:22:25.840
<v Maya>And he’s an early backer through effective altruism circles.

413
00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:28.860
<v Maya>What’s the real deal with his early stake, Alex?

414
00:22:29.410 --> 00:22:34.720
<v Alex>He put in $124 million in Series A, before Claude existed.

415
00:22:35.260 --> 00:22:36.920
<v Maya>He’s a doomer and a billionaire, huh?

416
00:22:37.260 --> 00:22:40.800
<v Alex>He sold Skype to eBay, then bet on safety‑first AI.

417
00:22:41.540 --> 00:22:43.730
<v Maya>Did he ever say why he’d back Anthropic?

418
00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:48.900
<v Alex>He said funding a safety‑conscious lab was the best way to keep a lid on risk.

419
00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:50.000
<v Maya>And he’s still holding?

420
00:22:50.430 --> 00:22:55.120
<v Alex>He’s one of the largest IPO winners; the return could be in the tens of billions.

421
00:22:55.840 --> 00:23:00.100
<v Alex>I think the IPO shows the market is still chasing hype, not safety.

422
00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:03.140
<v Maya>He’s betting on a lab that might not profit for years.

423
00:23:03.360 --> 00:23:04.060
<v Alex>I don’t buy it.

424
00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:06.900
<v Maya>But the valuation could make him a billionaire.

425
00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:09.860
<v Alex>It’s a gamble that could backfire if the tech stalls.

426
00:23:10.810 --> 00:23:13.200
<v Maya>Yet he’s a doomer, so maybe he’s hedging his bets.

427
00:23:13.810 --> 00:23:17.440
<v Alex>The real question is whether Anthropic can actually deliver.

428
00:23:17.910 --> 00:23:21.500
<v Maya>If they do, the return will be massive; if not, the hype will fade.

429
00:23:23.180 --> 00:23:29.720
<v Maya>Anthropic priced its IPO at $83, above the $70–78 range,

430
00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:31.960
<v Maya>valuing it at $1.5 trillion.

431
00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:33.600
<v Alex>83 bucks?

432
00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:34.240
<v Alex>That’s a jump.

433
00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:38.500
